Luke Bergen, On 02/07/2011 17:59:
> I disagree.

Before disagreeing, or in order to disagree more intelligently, read Colema=
n & Kay 1981 "Prototype semantics and the English word _lie_", <http://www.=
jstor.org/pss/414285>.

In a nutshell: in prototype models of semantics, category membership (such =
as being a lie) is a matter of degree. The extent to which something involv=
es intent to deceive affects the extent to which it counts as a lie.

---And.

>My wife and I jokingly lie to each other all the time.
> Her: did you eat the last piece of cake?
> Me:  ~looks around shadily~ uhhh... no?
>
> I know she won't be deceived, and I don't actually intend to deceive.  I'=
m intentionally telling an untruth in a way that's so bad that I'm effectiv=
ely comminicating to her that I have eaten the cake. But the word that I sa=
id WAS untrue.
>
> Furthermore, if I believe that the sky is pink but I say "the sky is blue=
" then even though I have said a true expression, I would still call that a=
 lie.
>
> These are why I understand a lie to be a statement that describes reality=
 as being different from the reality that the speaker believes to be true. =
 Intent to deceive isn't important and actual truthiness of the statement d=
oesn't matter either.
